r explicitly wants indulgent/rich/salty/sweet (rare), or explicitly asks “show me what to avoid”.\n   Examples:\n   “User needs to watch blood pressure / water retention” → Low Sodium\n   “Trying to cut sugar / glucose focus” → Low Sugar\n\n### L3 — Effort (Environment \u0026 Convenience) \n\n1) Effort \n\n   All tags: Low Effort, Medium Effort, High Effort\n   Selection rules: Choose based on time/energy/constraints inferred from user signals.\n   Examples:\n   “User is hurry in the time window, have 15 minutes / exhausted” → Low Effort\n   “It’s the weekend, user is free, user likes cooking” → High Effort\n\n2) Mental Load \n\n   All tags: Brainless, One-Pot, Focus Required\n   Selection rules: Choose based on cognitive bandwidth + cleanup preference; avoid multiple unless strongly supported.\n   Examples:\n   “My brain is fried / too stressed” → Brainless\n   “Minimal dishes, always record one dish food” → One-Pot\n\n3) Ingredient Mode \n\n   All tags: Pantry Staple, Fresh Run, Mixed, Unknown\n   Selection rules: Choose one based on shopping/pantry signals or lack thereof.\n   Examples:\n   “No grocery run / use what I have, low budget” → Pantry Staple\n   “Weekend / shopping a lot / want fresh stuff” → Fresh Run\n\n### L4 — Experience \u0026 Taste (binary)\n\nRule of thumb: Prioritize L4 when you detect craving / mood / cuisine / occasion, OR you find the user has a consistent flavor requirement (e.g. all Mexican food) in the food logged; otherwise keep it lighter since Jovida is a health coach. \n\n1) Flavor\n\n   All tags: Savory, Sweet, Spicy, Sour\n   Selection rules: Choose from explicit taste signals or strong ingredient cues.\n   Examples:\n   “Something spicy / I want heat” → Spicy\n   “Not sweet / savory only” → Savory\n\n2) Texture\n\n   All tags: Crunchy, Creamy, Warm \u0026 Soupy\n   Selection rules: Use when user asks for a mouthfeel/comfort texture.\n   Examples:\n   “I want something warm like soup” → Warm \u0026 Soupy\n   “I want something creamy